.\" bootcontrol(1) man page
.\" To preview: man -l docs/man/bootcontrol.1
.\" Install to /usr/share/man/man1/bootcontrol.1 via the .deb / .rpm rules.
.TH BOOTCONTROL 1 "2026-05-20" "BootControl 0.1.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
bootcontrol \- safe GRUB / systemd-boot / UKI configuration CLI

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bootcontrol
.RI [ COMMAND ]
.RI [ ARGS\&...\fR]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bootcontrol
is the command-line frontend for BootControl, a memory-safe Rust replacement
for tools like Grub Customizer. It talks to the
.BR bootcontrold (8)
daemon over D\-Bus; every write is authorised through Polkit and protected
by an ETag freshness check, so concurrent edits or stale-read overwrites
are rejected before they touch disk.

.B bootcontrol
never edits configuration files directly. All reads and writes round-trip
through the daemon, which holds the
.BR flock (2)
on the target file for the duration of the operation.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI get-config
Read the current GRUB configuration and ETag. Prints the parsed key=value
pairs and the 64-character SHA-256 ETag that callers must pass back on any
subsequent
.B set
call.
.TP
.BI get-etag
Print only the ETag of
.IR /etc/default/grub .
Convenience shortcut for scripts that have a cached key map and only need
to refresh the optimistic-concurrency token.
.TP
.BI set " KEY VALUE ETAG"
Atomically update a single GRUB variable. Requires Polkit authorisation
and the latest
.IR ETAG
returned by
.B get-config
or
.BR get-etag .
A stale
.IR ETAG
causes
.B org.bootcontrol.Error.StateMismatch
and no write happens. The daemon also enforces a payload blacklist
(\fBinit=\fR, \fBselinux=0\fR, etc.) and bails out on configs containing
complex Bash constructs.
.TP
.BI rescue
Scan attached block devices for installed root filesystems and print a
chroot recipe for the most likely one. Pure read-only operation — does
not modify any files. Mounts in the printed recipe are advisory; the user
runs them by hand.
.TP
.BR boot " " list
List all systemd-boot loader entries discovered under
.IR /boot/loader/entries/ .
Marks the current default with
.BR [default] .
.TP
.BI "boot set-default " ID " " ETAG
Set the default systemd-boot loader entry by ID (filename stem). Same
ETag-and-Polkit contract as
.BR set .
.TP
.BR cmdline " " get
Read
.IR /etc/kernel/cmdline ,
or on ostree atomic distros (Fedora Silverblue / Kinoite / IoT) read
.B rpm-ostree kargs
instead. The ETag is computed over the actual source so subsequent
.B add
/
.B remove
calls work uniformly across both backends.
.TP
.BI "cmdline add " PARAM " " ETAG
Append a kernel parameter. On rpm-ostree hosts this delegates to
.BR "rpm-ostree kargs --append" ;
on classic distros it edits
.IR /etc/kernel/cmdline
through the atomic write-rename pipeline. Both paths reject the blacklist.
.TP
.BI "cmdline remove " PARAM " " ETAG
Remove a kernel parameter. Same backend dispatch as
.BR "cmdline add" .
.TP
.BR help
Print the top-level help; pass a subcommand name to drill into that
subcommand's options.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BOOTCONTROL_BUS
Set to
.B session
to talk to the daemon on the D\-Bus session bus instead of the system bus
(used by the E2E test suite). Anything else / unset → system bus.
.TP
.B BOOTCONTROL_DEMO
Set to any non-empty value to use the in-process
.B MockBackend
instead of D\-Bus. The CLI returns plausible mock data for read commands
and refuses write commands. Useful on macOS or any host without a
bootcontrold installation.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Controls the
.B tracing-subscriber
verbosity filter
.RB ( trace ,
.BR debug ,
.BR info ,
.BR warn ,
.BR error ).

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/grub
The GRUB default configuration file. Edited atomically through the daemon
on every successful
.B set .
.TP
.I /boot/loader/entries/
systemd-boot loader entries. Edited through
.BR "boot set-default" .
.TP
.I /etc/kernel/cmdline
UKI kernel command line. Edited through
.BR "cmdline add/remove" .
.TP
.I /run/bootcontrold.sock
Socket the daemon listens on (socket-activated by systemd).

.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Generic failure (D\-Bus connection refused, invalid arguments, parser
error).
.TP
.B 2
A
.B BootControlError
the daemon could classify (\fBStateMismatch\fR, \fBPolkitDenied\fR,
\fBComplexBashDetected\fR, …). The structured D\-Bus error name appears
in the error message and is the canonical handle for scripted retries.

.SH EXAMPLES
Read the current config and capture the ETag for a follow-up write:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bootcontrol get-config
Active Backend: grub
ETag: 3f9c1aa8d72b...

Configuration:
GRUB_DEFAULT=0
GRUB_TIMEOUT=5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply a single change:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bootcontrol set GRUB_TIMEOUT 10 3f9c1aa8d72b...
.fi
.RE
.PP
List systemd-boot entries:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bootcontrol boot list
  arch [default]
  arch-fallback
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect kernel cmdline on a Fedora Silverblue host (transparently
delegates to
.BR "rpm-ostree kargs" ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ bootcontrol cmdline get
ETag: e2bba5...
Kernel Parameters (3 total):
  root=UUID=...
  quiet
  splash
.fi
.RE
.PP
Demo Mode (no daemon required):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ BOOTCONTROL_DEMO=1 bootcontrol get-config
.fi
.RE

.SH SECURITY MODEL
Every write call goes through:
.IP \(bu 4
.B Polkit
authorisation against an action in the
.B org.bootcontrol.*
namespace. The caller's UID is resolved through D\-Bus
.B GetConnectionUnixUser
\(em the caller cannot spoof it.
.IP \(bu 4
.B ETag
verification \(em a SHA-256 of the entire file at the moment of the
operation must match the value the caller cached. Concurrent
modification by another process (package manager, BTRFS rollback) is
rejected.
.IP \(bu 4
.B POSIX flock
on the target file before reading \(em closes the TOCTOU window between
read and write.
.IP \(bu 4
.B Payload blacklist
\(em \fBinit=\fR, \fBselinux=0\fR, \fBapparmor=0\fR, \fBmodule_blacklist=\fR
and other disabling-security parameters are refused before any write.
.IP \(bu 4
.B Atomic write
via \fIfile.tmp\fR + \fBrename(2)\fR; the original file is replaced in
one syscall.
.IP \(bu 4
.B Failsafe entry
re-injected into
.I /etc/bootcontrol/failsafe.cfg
after every successful GRUB write so a broken main entry still leaves a
known-good fallback.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bootcontrold (8),
.BR grub-mkconfig (8),
.BR systemd-boot (7),
.BR polkit (8),
.BR rpm-ostree (1)

